  "query_preview": "(Today through Thursday)...\nIssued at 145 AM EDT Fri Aug 14 2026\n\nThrough Mid-Morning...\n\nThunderstorms will continue to spread south and east across central \nIndiana during the predawn hours as an 850mb jet increases and \nprovides lift over a surface boundary. Shear and elevated \ninstability has already lead to large hail reports, and some \nisolated wind damage has occurred as well.\n\nThe severe threat will persist through dawn with little change \nexpected in the overall setup. \n\nWith precipitable water values near 2 inches, heavy rain will be a \nthreat with any storm. Potential training of storms will increase \nthe flooding threat. Unfortunately, ensembles focus the heaviest \nrain on the northern half of central Indiana, including areas \nalready hit hard by flooding. Current radar trends also support \nthose areas potentially seeing that heavy rain. Will continue to \nmessage the heightened threat.\n\nStorms should weaken diurnally after sunrise as the 850mb jet \nweakens, but that hasn't always been the case recently. Went high \nPoPs through around 12Z for the north 5/8 of the area or so, then \ndropped them back into the chance category. \n\nLate Morning through the Afternoon...\n\nWill continue to lower PoPs into early afternoon with no decent \nforcing and a worked over atmosphere. During the remainder of the \nafternoon, some recovery will occur so cannot rule out an isolated \nshower or storm. Kept slight chance PoPs around most areas. \n\nHighs today will depend on how well clouds clear out today and how \nfar south the thicker clouds initially get. For now have lowered \nguidance a bit north where clouds are likely to linger longest. Went \nwith highs in the lower 80s northeast to lower 90s southwest. These \nmay have to be adjusted later.\n\nWith the high moisture content of the atmosphere, heat indices may \nexceed 100 degrees southwest. \n\nTonight...\n\nSome upper energy may trigger isolated to scattered convection \nacross the northeast half to two-thirds of the area. Lows will be in \nthe upper 60s to middle 70s. If cloud cover remains relatively low \nsouthwest, some patchy fog may form.\n\nSaturday and Saturday Night...\n\nAn upper wave will ride along the end of the upper ridge and move \nthrough Lower Michigan and far northern Indiana on Saturday. This \nwill bring forcing to interact with decent moisture and produce some \nconvection. The bulk of this should remain north of central Indiana, \ncloser to the upper forcing. At the surface, a warm front will move \nnorth and bring some additional forcing. Will go mainly high chance \nPoPs north with lower PoPs south. These will linger into Saturday \nevening before diminishing as the upper wave exits. \n\nSome isolated severe storms are possible given expected instability \nand shear.\n\nThe passage of the warm front will bring in highs in the upper 80s \nto middle 90s, with the potential for heat indices of 100-105 \ndegrees.\n\nSunday and Sunday Night...\n\nSome additional upper energy will move through the upper flow, and \nthe upper flow will become more northwest. This will allow a surface \ncold front to move through the area, bringing more chances for \nshowers and thunderstorms.  \n\nHighs will generally be in the upper 80s to lower 90s. Depending on \nthe timing of convection, heat indices over 100 are possible again.\n\nMonday and Tuesday...\n\nSurface high pressure moving in behind the cold front will bring dry \nconditions to most areas during this period, with temperatures \ncloser to normal.\n\nWednesday and beyond...\n\nAnother surface front will move through Wednesday into Thursday. \nReturn flow on the back side of the high pressure will allow \nmoisture to return to the area, so will have some slight chance to \nchance PoPs around. Highest PoPs are expected on Wednesday, but some \nuncertainty remains on the timing.\n\nTemperatures will warm ahead of the front on Wednesday, but cooler \nair may return for Thursday into Friday.",
